// Lesson 8.6 — Boss Studio + Final Review
// Studio polish + semester review. CORRECTED: there is no boss door, the
// playthrough is the student's OWN route, and the project contains exactly
// ONE Coin instance - there is no coin economy to audit. The HUD is the
// Canvas + Text (TMP) the student built in 5.3: it lives in Start and reads
// the PLAYER's stats via StatManager. (The Overworld ships a Canvas of its
// own, wired to the lever - see 5.3.) There is no boss UI anywhere.
//
// NO LADDER LEVEL, ON PURPOSE. 8.6 is a studio day, not a build lesson: the
// page declares no ask banner and no Break It Down block, because
// src/data/asks.ts has no entry for 8.6 and verify-site fails any lesson that
// declares a ladder level without one. So this pass gives it the editor/code
// split, the pane names and the call naming, and nothing else - the same
// treatment 6.5 got as a converted studio day.
//
// STRUCTURE (this pass, editor/code split). The old "Studio Brief" was a
// paragraph and three unlabelled bullets, and it is now "In the Editor": the
// numbered steps name their pane (Project window for the clip, Inspector for
// the sprite colour and the text slot, Hierarchy for Main Camera and the
// Canvas, Game view for the run). The code half is new, and it carries the one
// decision in this studio that is made in a file rather than a field - the
// clear screen - as an architectural specification. Nothing else was added:
// the review questions, the two Socratic blocks, the single-coin inventory and
// the resources are unchanged in substance.
//
// GROUND TRUTH corrected in this pass (three questions presumed a build the
// student may not have made, and one named a place the number no longer
// appears):
//   - "Draw your boss's state machine from memory" presumed the 8.4 choice. A
//     state machine is one of THREE scheduling options there (a clock in
//     Update(), a coroutine, or the enum machine from 4.5), so the question now
//     asks for whichever one they built.
//   - "the boss, the arena and the boss door were not in the starter; you built
//     all three" presumed the exit was built, and 8.5 offers three ways for the
//     win to land, two of which build no exit object at all.
//   - "what did you do about it in your ground check (8.3)" credited the ground
//     check to 8.3. The ground check is 6.2's; 8.3 is where queriesHitTriggers
//     is taught and where the student chooses the tool that keeps it out of that
//     ground check, so the question cites both.
//   - "from the moment of contact to the number appearing in the Console": since
//     5.3 the coin count is read by HudController onto a Canvas in Start, and
//     2.1's Debug.Log is no longer the only place the total appears. The
//     question now asks for the trace to the number going up, which is true
//     whichever of the two the student is looking at.
//
// The checkpoint moved out of the Final Review block to the end of the page,
// where 2.4 and 6.5 keep theirs. It is a .checklist div and carries no answer
// box, so moving it is id-neutral.
//
// Answer-box ids are positional and persisted, so both socratic blocks keep
// their position, their order and their three questions (sc1:q1..q3 in the
// editor half, sc2:q1..q3 in the review). No question moved between blocks and
// no block was added, so no id changed. No `what: ''` line exists here - this
// lesson renders no walkthrough, and no ladder level and no title was touched.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>8.6 — Boss Studio + Final Review</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Your boss works (<a href="8.5.html">8.5</a>), and the semester's final is a normal B day — a
    normal B day where you ship the game you have been building since September. Today is split
    in two: polish in the studio, then the review that makes sure you can <em>talk</em> about
    every decision you made, out loud, without notes.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">In the Editor</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        <strong>Grade yourself against your own spec first.</strong> Open <code>BOSS_SPEC.md</code>
        from <a href="8.1.html">8.1</a> and read it line by line next to the running game. Every
        attack has a telegraph the player can see? Every phase change is visible within a second?
        The arena is the shape you drew? Write the gaps down before you fix any of them — a spec
        you never check against the build is a wish, not a specification.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>Then the three universal upgrades.</strong> Each one is optional, and each one has
        a real cost in a project this size:
    </p>

    <ol>
        <li>
            <strong>Project window →</strong> nothing in this project makes a sound yet, so there is
            no clip to drag in — import one of your own into <code>Assets</code> first. Then
            <strong>Inspector → Add Component → AudioSource</strong> on the object that should make
            the noise (open the attack prefab itself to put the swing sound on every instance), and
            drag the clip into the AudioSource's slot. Audio is half of "feel" and none of
            "mechanics", and it is the first audio in this project — budget for learning the
            component rather than assuming it is one line.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Inspector → Sprite Renderer → Color</strong> on the boss for the hit flash,
            or an <strong>Animator</strong> state if you would rather flash with an animation. Test
            it against the telegraph you already tuned: a flash that lands on the same frame as the
            wind-up is a tell competing with a tell.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Hierarchy → Main Camera → Inspector →</strong> the follow script from
            <code>Assets/Scripts/Package Scripts</code> for the camera nudge on the charge. Be
            careful here: that script is finished and it is not yours, so a shake has to work
            <em>with</em> somebody else's follow logic rather than around it.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Hierarchy → the Canvas in <code>Start (index 0)</code> → Inspector →</strong> the
            text slot on <code>HudController</code>, if the message belongs on the HUD you already
            own. If it belongs in the arena instead, you are building a second Canvas:
            <strong>right-click → UI → Text - TextMeshPro</strong> makes the Canvas, the text object
            and an EventSystem together. Which one it is, is the decision in the code half — make it
            there before you build either.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Game view →</strong> one full run, from the first control you ever wrote to the
            fight resolving, with a notes file open. Then <strong>GitHub Desktop →</strong> tag the
            build. The playable build is the portfolio.
        </li>
    </ol>

    <p>
        <strong>Ship-freeze rules for the review:</strong> no new attacks, no new systems, no
        "just one more mechanic." New things break old things, and the build you demo has to be
        the most boring build you have made all year.
    </p>

    <div class="callout info">
        <strong>One honest inventory before you polish anything.</strong> This project contains
        exactly <strong>one</strong> Coin instance in the entire game. There is no coin economy,
        no shop, and nothing that spends a coin. That is not a failure — it is the truth about
        what you built, and the review will go better if you can say it before anyone else does.
        Decide now: do you place more coins and give them a purpose, or do you leave the single
        coin and describe it accurately as a demonstration of the pickup system? Both are
        defensible. "I did not notice" is not.
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: What Did You Actually Ship?</h3>
        <ol>
<li>Name the one thing in your game that a stranger would remember after playing it. Is it the boss, the cave, or something you built in an earlier unit?</li>
<li>Your project ships one Coin. Where is it, what does collecting it prove, and what is the honest one-sentence description of your game's economy?</li>
<li>Which system in your game is the most fragile — the one most likely to break if you touched it? What does leaving it alone cost you?</li>
</ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">In the Code</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        One upgrade on that list is a file decision rather than a field, and it is the only code
        this studio asks for. The rest of the polish is an Inspector slot.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: the clear screen.</strong>
        <ul>
            <li>Which file owns the message — the boss's death path, the HUD, or the listener on the event you wired in <a href="8.5.html">8.5</a>? Pick one and say what the other two would have to know that they do not.</li>
            <li>The HUD you own is a Canvas in <code>Start (index 0)</code> reading the <em>player's</em> stats through <code>StatManager</code>, and the boss dies in the arena. What would it take for that HUD to hear about a boss in a different scene?</li>
            <li>If you print a line instead, who is it for — the player, or a tester with the Console open? Say which, honestly, then decide whether that is enough for a final build.</li>
            <li>If you build the second Canvas, what does the player see after a wipe sends them back to <code>Start (index 0)</code> — and are both Canvases reading the same numbers, or two different ones?</li>
            <li>The ship-freeze applies to code too, so this is the last script you write this semester. Can you test it inside the single run above — and if not, is that a reason to print a line and stop?</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity assessment">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Final Review</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        The final is a normal B day: your game, open, playable, and a conversation about it.
        Practise that conversation — answer each of these in about thirty seconds, out loud,
        without looking anything up.
    </p>
    <ol>
        <li>Trace collecting a coin from the moment of contact to the number going up. Name every
            method it passes through — and say which object the number now lives on, and why it
            moved there (<a href="2.1.html">2.1</a>, <a href="5.3.html">5.3</a>).</li>
        <li>Draw whatever schedules your boss's attacks, from memory — a state machine with the
            transition condition on every arrow, a coroutine in order, or the clock in
            <code>Update()</code> (<a href="4.5.html">4.5</a>, <a href="8.4.html">8.4</a>).</li>
        <li>Explain <code>Time.deltaTime</code> to someone who has never written code
            (<a href="1.5.html">1.5</a>).</li>
        <li>Why is your jump "tight"? Name the three values you tuned
            (<a href="6.3.html">6.3</a>).</li>
        <li>Where did you use the cooldown pattern? Count every instance in your game — the
            turret, then the enemy or the hazard, then your swing, then any attack the boss
            telegraphs.</li>
        <li>Where does the boss's health live, and where does the player's? Explain the
            difference in one sentence using the word <em>lifetime</em>
            (<a href="8.2.html">8.2</a>).</li>
        <li>What does <code>Physics2D.queriesHitTriggers</code> default to, and which tool did you
            use to stop it corrupting your ground check from <a href="6.2.html">6.2</a>
            (<a href="8.3.html">8.3</a>)?</li>
        <li>If you built a UnityEvent on your boss's death, what does it decouple? If you did
            not, say what you did instead and what it cost you
            (<a href="5.2.html">5.2</a>, <a href="8.5.html">8.5</a>).</li>
        <li>Which beat of your cave killed the most playtesters, and what is the data behind that
            claim (<a href="7.7.html">7.7</a>)?</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Inherit, Extend, Create</h3>
        <ol>
<li>Your game is a program built by forking a starter. What did you <strong>inherit</strong> unchanged, what did you <strong>extend</strong> by turning its knobs, and what did you <strong>create</strong> from nothing?</li>
<li>Which of the three categories is the largest in your project — and is that the answer you expected in September?</li>
<li>Your boss was not in the starter, and neither was the arena it fights in. Where is the line between "using a library" and "authorship" — and which side of it does your boss fall on?</li>
</ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="checklist">
    <h3>Checkpoint — the semester, complete:</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>One full run, from first control to the fight resolving, played and bug-listed</li>
        <li>Spec graded line by line; every remaining gap has a reason, not an excuse</li>
        <li>All nine review answers said out loud, without notes</li>
        <li>The single-coin fact known and described accurately, not discovered by someone else</li>
        <li>The clear-screen decision made in a file, and tested in the run</li>
        <li>Repo tagged and pushed — the playable build is the portfolio</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-08/index.html">Unit 8: Boss Fight</a> — the ship checklist</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — everything you used this semester, on one page</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="9.1.html">9.1 — Binary: How Numbers Live in Memory</a>, the next unit, where you find out what your <code>int</code> health actually is.</p>
</div>`;
